/**
 * @defgroup receiver_tank_calculator Receiver Tank Calculator
 * @ingroup compressed_air_assessments
 * @brief Sizes compressed air receiver tanks and calculates usable air capacity.
 * @details This calculator provides four engineering methods for sizing receiver tanks
 *          in a compressed air system and a standalone method for computing the usable
 *          air capacity of an existing tank. All formulas use U.S. customary units.
 *
 * A **receiver tank** (also called an air receiver or storage tank) is a pressure vessel
 * that stores compressed air between the compressor and the distribution system. It
 * serves several purposes:
 *
 * - Buffers short-duration demand peaks so the compressor does not short-cycle.
 * - Provides a reserve of compressed air when demand momentarily exceeds supply.
 * - Dampens pressure pulsations from reciprocating compressors.
 * - Separates moisture and oil carried over from the compressor.
 *
 * Choosing the correct tank volume is critical: an undersized tank causes excessive
 * compressor cycling and pressure drops; an oversized tank wastes capital and floor space.
 *
 * The calculation methods are:
 * 1. **Usable Capacity** – determines the volume of standard cubic feet actually available
 *    between two operating pressures for an existing or proposed tank.
 * 2. **General** – the simplest sizing method; computes the tank volume needed to supply a
 *    known air demand within an allowable pressure drop.
 * 3. **Dedicated Storage** – sizes a tank that must independently supply a sustained flow
 *    for a defined time interval by drawing down from a stored pressure.
 * 4. **Metered Storage** – extends Dedicated Storage by accounting for a metering (needle)
 *    valve that continuously replenishes the tank during the demand event; also computes
 *    the tank refill time after the event.
 * 5. **Bridging Compressor Reaction Delay** – sizes a tank that must supply air during
 *    the time the pressure signal propagates from a demand event back to the compressor
 *    room and the compressor responds.
 *
 * Relevant formulas are documented below.
 *
 * @heading{Usable Air Capacity}
 * @copydoc receiver_tank_usable_capacity_formula
 *
 * @heading{General Method}
 * @copydoc receiver_tank_general_size_formula
 *
 * @heading{Dedicated Storage Method}
 * @copydoc receiver_tank_dedicated_storage_size_formula
 *
 * @heading{Metered Storage Method — Tank Size}
 * @copydoc receiver_tank_metered_storage_size_formula
 *
 * @heading{Metered Storage Method — Refill Time}
 * @copydoc receiver_tank_metered_storage_refill_formula
 *
 * @heading{Bridging Compressor Reaction Delay Method}
 * @copydoc receiver_tank_bridging_size_formula
 */

/**
 * @defgroup receiver_tank_usable_capacity_formula Usable Air Capacity Formula
 * @ingroup receiver_tank_calculator
 * @brief Volume of standard cubic feet of air available between two operating pressures.
 * @details The usable capacity is the difference between the air stored at the charging
 *          (inlet) pressure and the air remaining at the cut-out (outlet) pressure,
 *          expressed in standard cubic feet. The tank volume in gallons is first converted
 *          to cubic feet, then scaled by the pressure differential relative to atmospheric
 *          pressure.
 *
 * @formula{receiver-tank-usable-capacity;
 *   V_{usable} = \frac{V_{tank}}{k_{gal}} \cdot \frac{P_{in} - P_{out}}{P_{atm}}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{V_{usable}; Usable air storage capacity; \scf}
 * @symrow{V_{tank}; Tank volume; \gallon}
 * @symrow{k_{gal}; Gallons per cubic foot (7.48); \gallon\per\cubicFoot}
 * @symrow{P_{in}; Charging (inlet) pressure; \psi}
 * @symrow{P_{out}; Cut-out (outlet) pressure; \psi}
 * @symrow{P_{atm}; Atmospheric pressure (14.7 psia at sea level); \psi}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup receiver_tank_general_size_formula General Method Tank Size Formula
 * @ingroup receiver_tank_calculator
 * @brief Sizes a receiver tank from a known air demand and allowable pressure drop.
 * @details The General method assumes the entire air demand must come from stored
 *          compressed air. The formula derives from the ideal gas law applied to the
 *          pressure differential across the tank: the tank must hold enough air at
 *          atmospheric pressure to satisfy the demand while keeping the pressure drop
 *          within the specified limit.
 *
 * @formula{receiver-tank-general-size;
 *   V_{tank} = Q_{demand} \cdot \frac{P_{atm}}{\Delta P} \cdot k_{gal}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{V_{tank}; Required receiver tank size; \gallon}
 * @symrow{Q_{demand}; Air demand drawn from the tank; \cubicFoot}
 * @symrow{P_{atm}; Atmospheric pressure; \psi}
 * @symrow{\Delta P; Allowable pressure drop; \psi}
 * @symrow{k_{gal}; Gallons per cubic foot (7.48); \gallon\per\cubicFoot}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup receiver_tank_dedicated_storage_size_formula Dedicated Storage Method Tank Size Formula
 * @ingroup receiver_tank_calculator
 * @brief Sizes a tank that must supply a sustained flow for a defined duration from stored pressure.
 * @details The Dedicated Storage method is used when a process requires a fixed flow rate
 *          for a known duration and the compressor cannot keep up. The tank is sized to
 *          provide that flow over the demand period while the pressure falls from the
 *          initial (fully charged) to the final (minimum acceptable) value.
 *
 * @formula{receiver-tank-dedicated-storage-size;
 *   V_{tank} = \frac{k_{gal} \cdot t_{demand} \cdot Q_{flow} \cdot P_{atm}}{P_1 - P_2}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{V_{tank}; Required receiver tank size; \gallon}
 * @symrow{k_{gal}; Gallons per cubic foot (7.48); \gallon\per\cubicFoot}
 * @symrow{t_{demand}; Duration of the air demand event; \minute}
 * @symrow{Q_{flow}; Required air flow rate during the demand event; \cubicFoot\per\minute}
 * @symrow{P_{atm}; Atmospheric pressure; \psi}
 * @symrow{P_1; Initial tank pressure (fully charged); \psi}
 * @symrow{P_2; Final tank pressure (minimum acceptable); \psi}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup receiver_tank_metered_storage_size_formula Metered Storage Method Tank Size Formula
 * @ingroup receiver_tank_calculator
 * @brief Sizes a tank when flow is regulated through a metering (needle) valve.
 * @details The Metered Storage method extends the Dedicated Storage method for the case
 *          where a needle valve or flow-control orifice continuously supplies air to the
 *          downstream process at a controlled rate @math{Q_{metered}} during the demand
 *          event. Only the net demand @math{(Q_{flow} - Q_{metered})} draws from the
 *          stored volume, so the required tank is smaller than the Dedicated Storage size
 *          for the same scenario.
 *
 * @formula{receiver-tank-metered-storage-size;
 *   V_{tank} = \frac{k_{gal} \cdot t_{demand} \cdot (Q_{flow} - Q_{metered}) \cdot P_{atm}}{P_1 - P_2}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{V_{tank}; Required receiver tank size; \gallon}
 * @symrow{k_{gal}; Gallons per cubic foot (7.48); \gallon\per\cubicFoot}
 * @symrow{t_{demand}; Duration of the air demand event; \minute}
 * @symrow{Q_{flow}; Required air flow rate during the demand event; \cubicFoot\per\minute}
 * @symrow{Q_{metered}; Metering valve (needle valve) flow rate; \cubicFoot\per\minute}
 * @symrow{P_{atm}; Atmospheric pressure; \psi}
 * @symrow{P_1; Initial tank pressure (fully charged); \psi}
 * @symrow{P_2; Final tank pressure (minimum acceptable); \psi}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup receiver_tank_metered_storage_refill_formula Metered Storage Method Refill Time Formula
 * @ingroup receiver_tank_calculator
 * @brief Time required to refill the receiver tank after a metered storage demand event.
 * @details After a demand event empties the tank from @math{P_1} to @math{P_2}, the
 *          metering valve continues to supply air at @math{Q_{metered}} while the system
 *          pressure recovers. The refill time is the time required to re-pressurize the
 *          tank volume @math{V_{cf}} from @math{P_2} back to @math{P_1} at the constant
 *          metered flow rate. The intermediate computation yields a time in minutes (since
 *          @math{Q_{metered}} is in cfm); multiplying by 60 converts to seconds.
 *
 * @formula{receiver-tank-metered-storage-refill;
 *   T_{refill} = \frac{60 \cdot V_{cf} \cdot (P_1 - P_2)}{Q_{metered} \cdot P_{atm}}
 * }
 *
 * where @math{V_{cf} = V_{tank} \cdot k_{cf}} is the tank volume in cubic feet.
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{T_{refill}; Tank refill time; \second}
 * @symrow{60; Seconds per minute conversion; \second\per\minute}
 * @symrow{V_{cf}; Tank volume in cubic feet; \cubicFoot}
 * @symrow{k_{cf}; Cubic feet per gallon (0.133681); \cubicFoot\per\gallon}
 * @symrow{P_1; Initial tank pressure (fully charged); \psi}
 * @symrow{P_2; Final tank pressure (minimum acceptable); \psi}
 * @symrow{Q_{metered}; Metering valve flow rate; \cubicFoot\per\minute}
 * @symrow{P_{atm}; Atmospheric pressure; \psi}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup receiver_tank_bridging_size_formula Bridging Compressor Reaction Delay Method Formula
 * @ingroup receiver_tank_calculator
 * @brief Sizes a tank to bridge the compressor reaction delay for a remote demand event.
 * @details When a sudden air demand occurs at a point distant from the compressor room,
 *          the pressure drop signal must travel back through the distribution piping to the
 *          compressor before the compressor can respond. During this transit time
 *          @math{t_{transit} = d_{pipe} / v_{air}} (in seconds), the receiver must supply
 *          all the required air. The formula converts the transit time to an equivalent
 *          tank volume using the air demand flow rate, pressure ratio, and the
 *          gallon-to-cubic-foot conversion.
 *
 * @formula{receiver-tank-bridging-size;
 *   V_{tank} = \frac{d_{pipe}}{v_{air}} \cdot \frac{Q_{demand}}{60} \cdot \frac{P_{atm}}{\Delta P} \cdot k_{gal}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{V_{tank}; Required receiver tank size; \gallon}
 * @symrow{d_{pipe}; Distance from demand event to compressor room; \foot}
 * @symrow{v_{air}; Speed of compressed air in distribution piping; \foot\per\second}
 * @symrow{Q_{demand}; Air demand at the event location; \cubicFoot\per\minute}
 * @symrow{60; Seconds per minute conversion; \second\per\minute}
 * @symrow{P_{atm}; Atmospheric pressure; \psi}
 * @symrow{\Delta P; Allowable pressure drop at the demand event; \psi}
 * @symrow{k_{gal}; Gallons per cubic foot (7.48); \gallon\per\cubicFoot}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * @note The factor @math{d_{pipe} / v_{air}} yields the signal transit time in seconds.
 *       Dividing @math{Q_{demand}} by 60 converts from cfm to ft³/s so the product of
 *       transit time × flow rate gives a volume in ft³, which is then scaled to gallons.
 */
